    <h1>Terms</h1>
    <p class="legal-lead">Portico helps people enjoy media they are authorized to use on servers they control or have been invited to access. These terms set a few practical rules for keeping that experience safe and reliable.</p>

    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> August 24, 2026</p>

    <h2>About these terms</h2>
    <p>These terms apply to the Portico website, Portico-hosted account and connection services, and Portico software and applications. By creating a Portico Account or using a hosted Portico service, you agree to these terms. Software distributed under an open-source or other license also remains subject to the license included with that software.</p>

    <h2>Your server and media</h2>
    <p>A Portico Server is operated by its owner, not by Portico. Server owners decide what media is available, who may connect, and which permissions each person receives. Owners are responsible for their server administration, security, backups, media, and compliance with the laws and agreements that apply to them. People invited to a server are responsible for using that access appropriately.</p>
    <p>Portico does not provide media, licenses, or permission to copy, share, stream, or otherwise use third-party content. You may use Portico only with media and services you are authorized to access.</p>

    <h2>What Portico Cloud does</h2>
    <p>Portico Cloud provides a limited control plane for Portico Accounts, sign-in, server claiming, invitations and memberships, device setup, direct-connection coordination, and related security and reliability functions.</p>
    <p>Portico Cloud does not host, relay, cache, transform, inspect, or monitor your media. It does not receive ordinary library browsing, playback, or server API traffic, and it does not collect library contents, media filenames, filesystem paths, playback history, watchlists, ratings, queues, local searches, or streaming details. After a connection is established, your device communicates directly with the independently operated Portico Server.</p>

    <h2>Your account</h2>
    <p>Provide accurate account information, protect your credentials and recovery methods, and keep your devices reasonably secure. You are responsible for activity authorized through your account unless you promptly report suspected compromise. Portico may require additional verification or revoke sessions when reasonably necessary to protect an account or the service.</p>

    <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
    <p>Do not use Portico to infringe intellectual-property rights, bypass access controls, gain unauthorized access, distribute malware, harass or exploit others, attack systems, interfere with Portico services, automate abusive traffic, or violate applicable law. Do not misrepresent your identity or your authority to operate or grant access to a server.</p>
    <p>Portico is not intended to be a public catalogue, commercial redistribution service, or source of media. Private sharing does not by itself create permission to use content.</p>

    <h2>Reports and good-faith review</h2>
    <p>Copyright and other abuse concerns may be sent to <a href="mailto:abuse@getportico.tv">abuse@getportico.tv</a>. Our <a href="/abuse/">Abuse page</a> explains what to include and how we review a report.</p>
    <p>Portico reviews sufficiently specific reports in good faith. Depending on the circumstances, we may ask for more information, notify the relevant account or server operator, preserve records where lawfully required, or limit the Portico-hosted account, discovery, invitation, membership, or routing services involved. We may suspend or end hosted access for repeated or egregious violations. Where practical and appropriate, we provide notice and an opportunity to correct or dispute a report.</p>
    <p>Because Portico does not possess media stored on independently operated servers, it ordinarily cannot inspect or remove those files. A report may be more effectively directed to the server operator or network provider that actually hosts or transmits the material.</p>

    <h2>Software and updates</h2>
    <p>Portico software, documentation, downloads, and hosted features will evolve. Updates may add, change, or remove functionality, including functionality needed for security, compatibility, or reliable operation. Review release notes and keep backups before changing a production server. Do not defeat security mechanisms or use unsupported modifications to interfere with Portico or other systems.</p>

    <h2>Availability</h2>
    <p>We work to make Portico dependable, but no software or online service is uninterrupted or error-free. Hosted services may be unavailable during maintenance, security response, provider failures, or events outside our reasonable control. Local server use and previously established direct connections may continue independently where the product supports them.</p>

    <h2>Limiting or ending access</h2>
    <p>You may stop using Portico and close your Portico Account through the available account tools or by contacting us. Portico may limit or end access to hosted services when reasonably necessary to address a material breach of these terms, a credible security or legal risk, non-payment for a paid service, or harm to Portico, its providers, or other people. We will provide notice when it is practical and safe to do so.</p>

    <h2>Third-party services</h2>
    <p>Portico may depend on operating systems, app stores, certificate authorities, email delivery, networks, and other third-party services. Their own terms and privacy practices apply to their services. Portico is not responsible for independently operated servers or third-party products, content, availability, or conduct.</p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>Our <a href="/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a> describes the limited information Portico processes and the media information Portico Cloud does not receive. By using Portico-hosted services, you acknowledge that processing as described there.</p>

